// THE MACHINE-WIDTH UInt8 <-> Int CROSSING. Origin: review 95669 on PR #16520. DESIGN.md states the
// limit in the model itself (`UInt8` is `Compose<UInt, MachineWidth<8>>`, phantom and transparent to
// `Int` across a formal; the worked example is a floor receipt observing `256` cross a `UInt8`
// parameter in `test.claim.machine_word_witness`), and the ruling decides the intended model, but
// neither claims a route -- this row is where the crossing exerts pressure.
// The revision at which this unit's GPU rows entered the model, and the two receipts the two rows below are
// made against. The digests are the committed files under
// artifacts/host-observations/ken-gpu-read-2026-11-18/ (SHA256SUMS in that directory lists all five files).
// The revision the two Spark-endpoint rows below were read against: main, after gunbc.fleet.fleet_intent_network's
// srv9 retirement (#16712) landed and before the srv14/srv11 address move in PR #16655.
// The revision at which the three stale declarations were deleted and this row was integrated. It is a branch
// revision, not a main revision: main's merge revision supersedes it when the change lands, and the account says so
// rather than quoting a main sha that does not exist yet.
// THREE ENDPOINT ROWS ANSWER, THE TOPOLOGY ROSTER DOES NOT. srv10_host_lan_endpoint, srv11_host_lan_endpoint and
// srv12_host_lan_endpoint each name a LAN address for a Spark unit, and none of the three is carried in
// gunbc.fleet.fleet_intent_network::endpoints. Two places in one module therefore answer one question
// differently: the row says the unit has an address, and the roster -- which is what
// gunbc.fleet.fleet_intent_network::host_lan_address folds over -- says it has none. Nothing in the corpus says
// which answer is meant: no row records those units as reserved rather than in service, and no row records the
// omission as deliberate.
